    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The build succeeds with all tests green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe change requires all rejection responses (400, 409, 429) to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\n**What I found:** Rejections in this OfficeFloor app are produced by escalation handlers in `src/main/java/.../rest/escalation/`. A `ProblemDetails` helper and RFC7807 bodies already existed for some handlers (403, 404, 500, and the bean-validation 400), but the nine domain-rejection handlers still returned plain `Map.of(\"errors\", ...)` JSON bodies:\n\n- **400:** `DisposableOwnerEmail`, `FutureRegistrationDate`, `InvalidOwnerEmail`, `InvalidOwnerPostcode`, `InvalidOwnerTelephone`, `MissingOwnerFields`\n- **409:** `CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateOwnerIdentity`\n- **429:** `DailyCreateLimit`\n\n**Changes made:**\n1. **`ProblemDetails.java`** \u2014 added an `asResponse(...)` helper that wraps a `ProblemDetail` in a `ResponseEntity` with the `Content-Type` set explicitly to `application/problem+json`. I verified via the Spring bytecode that `ProblemDetail` only auto-negotiates `application/problem+json` when the normal content negotiation yields nothing \u2014 under MockMvc's default `Accept: */*` it would otherwise serialize as plain `application/json`, so setting the header explicitly is what makes the media type deterministic.\n2. **The nine rejection handlers** \u2014 rewritten to build an RFC7807 `ProblemDetail` (unchanged HTTP status) via the shared helper, preserving the offending field names as an `errors` property so no information is lost.\n3. **`ValidationExceptionHandler`** (the other 400 rejection) \u2014 routed through the same `asResponse` helper so it too guarantees the `application/problem+json` content type.\n\nThe status codes are unchanged; only the bodies and content type changed. The new `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
